\title{Transcript Hashing and Duplex Sponges}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-20}

\p{
    There are two ways to derive the challenges and the deployed systems use the second one.
    Re-hashing the whole serialised transcript at every round is what the definition says and costs
    hash work quadratic in the transcript length. A duplex sponge keeps a state, absorbs each prover
    message into it, and squeezes each challenge out of it, so the work is linear and the state
    carries the dependence on everything absorbed so far. With [Poseidon](dhsorens-004H) the
    permutation is the dominant in-circuit cost of verifying a proof inside another proof, so the
    number of permutation calls is a parameter someone optimises, not a detail.
}

\p{
    Chiesa and Orrù analysed that object rather than the definition. Their Fiat–Shamir
    transformation is built on an ideal permutation, follows the duplex sponge paradigm, minimises
    permutation calls for given absorb and squeeze requirements, and comes with concrete bounds
    intended to set security parameters. Having an analysis of the deployed variant matters more
    than it sounds: the textbook argument is about #{H} applied to a transcript, the shipped code
    runs a sponge over a permutation, and the two are different constructions on different
    idealisations.
}

\p{
    The negative result is the load-bearing one. Indifferentiability is the property that licenses
    treating a sponge as a random oracle, and it is proved for the duplex construction; Chiesa and
    Orrù find it ill-suited for establishing the knowledge soundness and the zero knowledge of a
    non-interactive argument. So the standard bridge from "this mode of operation is sound" to "the
    compiled argument inherits its security" does not carry the two properties the argument is
    bought for, and a sponge-based system needs its own analysis rather than a citation to
    indifferentiability. This is not a break of anything; it is a gap where an implementer would
    reasonably have assumed there was a theorem.
}

\p{
    Their library, spongefish — the successor to nimue, now under arkworks — makes the resulting
    discipline an API. It is permutation-agnostic, ships Keccak and Poseidon, supports byte
    challenges with [grinding](dhsorens-003A) integrated into the verifier, and constructs a prover
    or verifier state only from a \code{DomainSeparator} composed of a protocol identifier, a
    mandatory session identifier, and the public instance. Requiring the instance to build the state
    is the structural fix for [weak Fiat–Shamir](dhsorens-0050): the statement is in the hash
    because the type says so, rather than because the implementer remembered.
}

\p{
    Serialisation is inside the security argument, not underneath it. Absorbing a field element
    means fixing an encoding, and two distinct transcripts that encode to the same byte string are a
    collision the proof assumed away — so canonical encodings and unambiguous absorb boundaries are
    soundness obligations. The sponge's own collision term comes from its capacity: a capacity of
    #{c} bits gives generic security around #{2^{c/2}}, which is why a 128-bit target wants a
    256-bit capacity and why the rate-capacity split is a parameter and not a performance knob. That
    term appears in the [bit-security accounting](dhsorens-004N) alongside the Merkle one.
}

\p{
    [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) formalises the sponge variant separately from the plain one, in
    \code{OracleReduction/FiatShamir/DuplexSponge}. \code{Defs.lean} defines the multi-round
    transform over a \code{CanonicalDuplexSponge}, with a challenge oracle combining a random oracle
    and a permutation oracle, a \code{deriveTranscriptDSFS} that reconstructs the full transcript
    from the messages by absorbing and squeezing, and \code{duplexSpongeFiatShamir} for provers,
    verifiers, and reductions. Domain separation is carried in the types: \code{HasMessageSize} and
    \code{HasChallengeSize} instances fix a size per round, so rounds are distinguishable by
    construction. Salt is deliberately absent — a salted protocol is obtained by treating the salt
    as the protocol's first message, which is the same trick [ArkLib's Salt.lean](dhsorens-0050)
    uses one level up.
}

\p{
    Sources: [A Fiat–Shamir Transformation From Duplex Sponges](https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/536)
    (Chiesa, Orrù, TCC 2025); [spongefish](https://github.com/arkworks-rs/spongefish);
    \code{ArkLib/OracleReduction/FiatShamir/DuplexSponge}.
}
